## Key findings

| # | Finding |
|---|---|
| 1 | **Movies dominate:** 69.7% of titles are movies and 30.3% are TV shows. Two-thirds of TV shows have only one season. |
| 2 | **A few countries dominate:** of 8,098 titles with a known country, the US has 40.0%, India 13.0% and the UK 7.9%. The top 3 hold 60.9% and the top 10 hold 78.2%. |
| 3 | **Growth boom, then a pause:** titles added per year grew about 68% a year from 2016 to 2019 (426 to 2,013), then fell 6.7% in 2020. 2021 is a partial year. |
| 4 | **Recent and adult-oriented:** 70.6% of titles were released in 2015 or later; adult-rated content is 45.6% and kids/family only 23.4%. |
| 5 | **Format differs by country:** South Korea (77% TV) and Japan (66% TV) are series-led; India is movie-led (92% movies). |
| 6 | **TV is gaining share of additions:** 25.0% in 2018 to 33.7% in 2021. |
| 7 | **Metadata gaps:** 688 titles have no usable country and 2,587 (29.4%) have no director. |

## Recommendations

1. **Grow series alongside movies** and track season-2 and season-3 conversion.
2. **Diversify beyond the US**, building on India, the UK, Canada, Japan, South Korea and France.
3. **Close the family/kids gap** (23.4% today); test a 25 to 30% target.
4. **Move from volume to curation** after the 2019 peak.
5. **Back local-language originals by format strength:** series from Korea and Japan, movies from India.
6. **Improve metadata quality** at source so country and director are never placeholders.
7. **Validate data before acting on outliers.** The apparent Pakistan spike (420 titles) was a placeholder artefact; the real figure is 19.

